## 🟥 FileName Globbing - Allows selection of files based on a pattern match. (Select which files to display)
* `ls *2014*` : ________________________________________
* `ls *201[34]*` : When square brackets are used during filename globbing (filtering). That means you're trying to match for one items inside of the bracket not both at the same time.
* `ls *[East,West]*` : Multiple items can be "globbed" or matched by using a comma to seperate each item.
* `ls *{2013..2015}*` : *{<x>..<x>}* = Formula to use filename globbing. Used to set the range to search in/filter -> like saying, show me any queries from a all the way to c. --> !!The contacts app is literally a perfect example. Lists your saved contacts by name starting the listing range from a all the way to . or z. Explanation of what the each symbol represents/is in chronological order (left to right as it actually reads)-->  * = Globbing range start point (the one before the opening "{"), then open with { and the first <x> represents the value that you'd want to start at then seperate with .. and the value you want it to end at in the second <x> then close with } and the * at the end represents where to stop globbing (end point).
- Note for Filename Globbing: if you do not include the 2nd asterisk (globbing end point symbol) then it will not have an end point set. (Do not need an * if the second <x> globbing item are the final characters of the filename.
* `ls [HC][A-Z][0-9][0-9]???` : ls queries starting with H and C, for first letter, and uppercase letter in A to Z range for second letter of name, and any # from 1 through 9 for third character. 

